####################################################################################
# The machines that run the engine. One address per line; # comments, blanks ignored.
#
# Copy it to `hosts` (which is gitignored -- it holds your real addresses) and put
# your machines in. Everything else about them is in .env: they all run the same
# model, with the same engine and the same accelerators.
#
# NODES_PER_INSTANCE in .env decides what these machines add up to. Four machines of
# eight cards is 32 cards either way:
#   NODES_PER_INSTANCE=1  ->  four instances of 8 cards (the default)
#   NODES_PER_INSTANCE=4  ->  one instance of 32 cards
# They are taken in the order they appear here, so with NODES_PER_INSTANCE=2 the
# first two lines are one instance and the next two are another. The first machine
# of each instance is rank 0: it is the one that serves the API and the only one the
# routers are told about -- the rest hold shards and answer nothing.
#
# A second column is only needed when SSH and the data network are different
# addresses -- then it is the address the routers and the other machines of the
# instance use, and the first column is just how you log in:
#
#   node-a   10.0.0.11
####################################################################################

# With NODES_PER_INSTANCE=1, each of these machines is its own instance and both
# serve the same route:
10.0.0.21
10.0.0.22

# The same two machines where SSH does not go to their data network -- the second
# column is the address the routers and the other machines of an instance use:
# node-a   10.0.0.21
# node-b   10.0.0.22
